course: biology
lesson: 23
title: Bears
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: There are eight kinds of bear in the world, from the huge polar bear on the Arctic sea ice to the small sun bear in the rainforests of Southeast Asia, and each one has four strong legs, a big nose and a body built around finding food.
explain: Most bears eat almost anything: berries, nuts, roots, insects, fish and meat, so a grizzly at a waterfall in autumn snaps up leaping salmon to get fat before winter.
explain: Then it crawls into a den and sleeps for months without eating, drinking or going to the toilet, living on that fat, and in midwinter a mother gives birth to tiny cubs about the size of a guinea pig.
explain: A polar bear looks white, but each hair is see-through and hollow and its skin is black, and the giant panda eats almost only bamboo, gripping the stems with a thumb-like bump on its wrist.
explain: Winnie the Pooh and Yogi Bear are friendly and steal honey and picnic baskets; real bears do raid bins and love honey, but a bear used to human food becomes dangerous, so never feed one and keep far away.
fact: There are eight species of bear, and the koala is not one of them but a marsupial.
fact: Polar bears are excellent swimmers that can swim for hours or even days between pieces of sea ice, and scientists class them as marine mammals.
fact: A polar bear's hairs are see-through and hollow, look white and trap warmth, and its skin underneath is black; in warm zoos tiny green algae sometimes grow inside the hollow hairs and turn the fur green.
fact: Polar bears wait beside breathing holes in the ice to catch seals, and can smell a seal under snow from about a kilometre away.
fact: Brown bears, including grizzlies, catch salmon at waterfalls in autumn to build up fat before winter.
fact: In winter, when food is scarce, many bears sleep in a den for months to save energy, living on stored fat without eating, drinking or going to the toilet, while their heartbeat slows a lot.
fact: Black and brown bear cubs are born in the den in midwinter and are only about the size of a guinea pig.
fact: Giant pandas eat almost only bamboo, up to about 12 to 38 kilograms a day, and grip it with an extra wrist bone that works like a thumb; their teeth and tummy are built like a meat-eater's, and bamboo gives so little energy that they eat for up to about 14 hours a day.
fact: The sun bear is the smallest bear; it lives in Southeast Asian rainforests and has a tongue up to about 25 centimetres long for honey and insects.
fact: Most bears are omnivores, and their sense of smell is many times better than a dog's.
fact: Bears that get used to human food often become dangerous, so parks use bear-proof bins and tell visitors never to feed bears.
fact: A brown bear can run about 50 kilometres an hour in short bursts, faster than the fastest human sprinter, and a mother with cubs is especially dangerous, so back away slowly and never go between a mother and her cubs.
fact: The teddy bear is named after US President Theodore Roosevelt, who in 1902 refused to shoot a bear tied up on a hunt.
exercise: How do polar bears get from one piece of sea ice to another? | They swim, sometimes for hours or days | They wait for the ice to freeze together | They jump across like goats | They ride on the backs of whales
exercise: What colour is a polar bear's skin under its fur? | Black | White | Pink | Pale blue
exercise: What are a polar bear's hairs really like? | See-through and hollow | Pure white and solid | Grey and woolly | Green from algae in the wild
exercise: Why does a polar bear wait beside a hole in the ice? | Seals must come up there to breathe | It is waiting for the ice to melt | It is drinking the sea water | It is watching out for other bears on the ice
exercise: Why does a grizzly bear catch so many salmon in autumn? | To get very fat before its winter sleep | To store them in its den for the long winter | To feed them to its hungry cubs in the river | Because salmon is the only thing it eats
exercise: What does a bear in its den do all winter? | It sleeps and lives on its stored fat | It eats the food it buried back in autumn | It wakes up every single night to go and hunt | It goes out and catches fish under the ice
exercise: About how big is a newborn black or brown bear cub? | About the size of a guinea pig | About the size of a full-grown sheep | About the size of a grown sheepdog | About the size of a small peanut
exercise: When are black and brown bear cubs born? | In midwinter, in the den | In late summer, by a river | In spring, out in the open | Any time of year, in a tree
exercise: What does a giant panda eat almost all the time? | Bamboo | Fish | Honey | Seals
exercise: How does a panda grip bamboo stems? | With an extra wrist bone that works like a thumb | With its long sticky tongue | With its back feet only | It can't grip them at all, so it eats them lying flat
exercise: Which is the smallest bear? | The sun bear | The polar bear | The panda | The black bear
exercise: Why does a sun bear have a tongue up to 25 centimetres long? | To reach honey and insects inside logs and nests | To catch fish from the riverbank | To cool down in the hot rainforest | To clean the mud off its cubs
exercise: Why do bears raid rubbish bins at campsites? | Their superb noses smell the food, and they eat almost anything | They only eat plastic | They want to knock things over for fun | Bins are warm, dark and dry places where they can sleep all winter
exercise: What does a ranger tell visitors to do about bears? | Keep far away, never feed them and lock food away | Feed them so they stay friendly | Run up close to take a photo | Shout at them loudly and chase them off the path with a stick
exercise: About how fast can a brown bear run in a short burst? | About 50 km an hour | About 5 km an hour | About 150 km an hour | About 15 km an hour
exercise: Who is the teddy bear named after? | US President Theodore Roosevelt | The writer of Winnie the Pooh | A German toy maker called Teddy | Queen Victoria's favourite pet
test: lol my koala teddy is a bear right?? says so on the label | No, a koala is a marsupial, not a bear | Yes, koalas are the smallest bears in the whole world | Yes, it is a small kind of panda | No, koalas are a kind of monkey
test: In the cartoon Yogi Bear steals picnic baskets and everyone laughs. What happens to real bears that learn this? | They often become dangerous and may have to be moved or put down | They become tame pets for the campers | They forget it by the next week | Nothing, it is good for bears
test: Count them: a mother bear and two cubs come out of a den. How many legs is that? | 12 | 8 | 6 | 16
test: Why might a polar bear's hollow see-through hairs be useful in the Arctic? | They look white on snow and trap warmth | They let the bear see right through its own fur | They make the bear float on water like a balloon | They glow in the dark to help it find seals
test: If a zoo polar bear's fur turned green, what is the likely reason? | Tiny algae grew inside its hollow hairs | It ate too much spinach | Its skin changed colour from the terrible cold | Someone painted it as a joke
test: What would happen to a polar bear if the sea ice melted early each year? | It would have less time to hunt seals | It would catch more seals than ever before | It would simply switch to eating bamboo instead | Nothing, polar bears hunt only on land
test: My brother says bears hibernate cos they're lazy. what's the real reason? | Food is scarce in winter, so sleeping saves energy | Bears are bored in winter | Bears hate the feel of cold snow on their paws in winter | Their claws fall out every winter
test: Which is true about a giant panda's body? | It is built like a meat-eater but eats almost only bamboo | It has the stomach of a cow, with four parts, to digest the grass | It is a kind of raccoon, not a bear at all | It needs only a few leaves a day
test: shut up and stand still, there's a bear with cubs over there!! | Back away slowly and calmly, never go between a mother and cubs | Run as fast as you can, you'll beat it | Go closer to see the cubs | Throw your sandwich to make friends
test: Why are pandas busy eating for up to 14 hours a day? | Bamboo gives them very little energy, so they need loads | They eat slowly because they have no teeth | They only eat in the dark, when it is cooler and they feel safer | They are storing it in their cheeks
test: Why did a 1902 cartoon lead to the teddy bear? | Roosevelt refused to shoot a tied-up bear, and a toy maker made a bear named after him | Roosevelt kept a real bear called Teddy in the White House | Roosevelt invented the toy himself | A bear called Teddy saved the President
exam: Ascertain the number of extant species of the family of bears. | Eight | Three | Twenty | One
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the pelage and integument of the polar bear? | Its hairs are translucent and hollow, and its skin is black | Its hairs are solid white, and its skin is white | Its hairs are grey, and its skin is pink | It is devoid of hair, and its skin is white
exam: Identify the principal reason for which visitors to bear habitats are enjoined never to provide food to bears. | Bears habituated to human food frequently become dangerous | Human food is too costly for the park authorities to provide in quantity | Bears are unable to digest any food | Bears so fed cease to hibernate entirely
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the dietary habit of the giant panda. | It consumes bamboo almost exclusively | It consumes seals almost exclusively | It consumes honey almost exclusively | It consumes fish almost exclusively
